Not valid — no fix needed.

The premise is wrong. `pageSize` in `query.paginated` is **not** a cap on the total results — it's just the per-request page size. Looking at the implementation in [query.ts](src/Utils/request/query.ts#L156-L199), the function loops fetching successive pages and aggregates them into `items` until `items.length >= res.count` (it only stops early if `maxPages` is passed, which it isn't here):

```ts
while (hasNextPage) {
  const res = await query(route, { queryParams: { limit: pageSize, offset: page * pageSize, ... }})(...);
  items.push(...res.results);
  if (options?.maxPages && page >= options.maxPages - 1) hasNextPage = false;
  if (items.length >= res.count) hasNextPage = false;
  page++;
}
```

So `supplyDeliveries.results` holds **all** deliveries for the order, not at most `maxDatapointsPerUpsert`. If an order has more than `maxDatapointsPerUpsert` `in_progress` deliveries, "Select all" sets `selectedDeliveries.length > maxDatapointsPerUpsert`, making `isWithinUpsertLimit` false — which disables the action buttons and shows the warning Callout. That's exactly the guard's purpose, so it's live code, not dead.

(`selectedDeliveries.length <= results.length` is true, but `results.length` itself is uncapped, so nothing forces `selectedDeliveries.length <= maxDatapointsPerUpsert`.)
